1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,902
Sweetheart, yesterday you laughed at me.

2
00:00:03,082 --> 00:00:05,801
You opened my kitchen drawer and found

3
00:00:05,801 --> 00:00:07,160
forty rubber bands,

4
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:08,663
a hundred twist ties,

5
00:00:08,663 --> 00:00:11,096
and a stack of margarine tubs I've

6
00:00:11,096 --> 00:00:12,312
washed and saved.

7
00:00:12,492 --> 00:00:13,597
And you said,

8
00:00:13,597 --> 00:00:16,486
Grandma, why do you keep all this?

9
00:00:16,666 --> 00:00:18,291
And I laughed too.

10
00:00:18,471 --> 00:00:20,232
But then I thought about it all

11
00:00:20,232 --> 00:00:20,573
night.

12
00:00:20,753 --> 00:00:22,413
Because there's a reason.

13
00:00:22,593 --> 00:00:25,737
There's a reason for almost everything people

14
00:00:25,737 --> 00:00:26,505
like me do.

15
00:00:26,856 --> 00:00:28,942
People who grew up in the nineteen

16
00:00:28,942 --> 00:00:31,151
fifties are a little strange to you.

17
00:00:31,331 --> 00:00:34,036
We say things you don't understand.

18
00:00:34,216 --> 00:00:36,763
We do things that drive your mother

19
00:00:36,763 --> 00:00:37,200
crazy.

20
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:40,468
So tonight, I'm going to explain us.

21
00:00:40,648 --> 00:00:43,292
Eleven things about how we think,

22
00:00:43,292 --> 00:00:46,256
and exactly where each one came from.

23
00:00:46,436 --> 00:00:48,701
And maybe next time you'll laugh a

24
00:00:48,701 --> 00:00:49,501
little less.

25
00:00:49,681 --> 00:00:50,703
Maybe.

26
00:00:51,053 --> 00:00:52,214
Number one.

27
00:00:52,394 --> 00:00:53,822
We waste nothing.

28
00:00:54,002 --> 00:00:55,057
Not a crust,

29
00:00:55,057 --> 00:00:56,641
not a rubber band,

30
00:00:56,641 --> 00:00:58,576
not a piece of string.

31
00:00:58,756 --> 00:01:00,962
And that didn't start with us,

32
00:01:00,962 --> 00:01:01,403
honey.

33
00:01:01,583 --> 00:01:04,736
It started with our parents and grandparents,

34
00:01:04,736 --> 00:01:07,469
who lived through the Great Depression.

35
00:01:07,649 --> 00:01:10,190
Grandma Pearl stood in a bread line

36
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:12,804
in Mansfield in nineteen thirty-two.

37
00:01:12,984 --> 00:01:15,945
She raised two boys on almost nothing.

38
00:01:16,125 --> 00:01:18,422
And she raised my mother to be

39
00:01:18,422 --> 00:01:19,418
the same way,

40
00:01:19,418 --> 00:01:21,256
and my mother raised me.

41
00:01:21,607 --> 00:01:22,649
In our house,

42
00:01:22,649 --> 00:01:25,136
bacon grease went into a can on

43
00:01:25,136 --> 00:01:26,901
the stove for cooking.

44
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:29,748
Old shirts became dish rags,

45
00:01:29,748 --> 00:01:31,272
then floor rags.

46
00:01:31,452 --> 00:01:34,285
Bread heels went into bread pudding.

47
00:01:34,465 --> 00:01:36,995
Grandma Pearl used to say,

48
00:01:36,995 --> 00:01:37,969
use it up,

49
00:01:37,969 --> 00:01:39,137
wear it out,

50
00:01:39,137 --> 00:01:40,207
make it do,

51
00:01:40,207 --> 00:01:41,570
or do without.

52
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:44,638
Every child of the fifties heard those

53
00:01:44,638 --> 00:01:45,094
words.

54
00:01:45,274 --> 00:01:48,107
That's why I save the margarine tubs.

55
00:01:48,287 --> 00:01:50,431
Some part of me still thinks the

56
00:01:50,431 --> 00:01:52,977
Depression could come back on Tuesday.

57
00:01:53,327 --> 00:01:56,175
We ate everything on our plates,

58
00:01:56,175 --> 00:01:56,531
too.

59
00:01:56,711 --> 00:01:58,266
My mother would say,

60
00:01:58,266 --> 00:02:01,222
there are children starving in Europe,

61
00:02:01,222 --> 00:02:02,466
and I would say,

62
00:02:02,466 --> 00:02:04,722
then send them my lima beans.

63
00:02:04,902 --> 00:02:07,119
That got me sent to my room.

64
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:09,900
Leftovers became Monday's soup.

65
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:12,773
Sour milk became pancakes.

66
00:02:12,953 --> 00:02:13,879
To this day,

67
00:02:13,879 --> 00:02:16,655
honey, I cannot scrape food into the

68
00:02:16,655 --> 00:02:19,817
garbage without feeling a little pinch in

69
00:02:19,817 --> 00:02:20,512
my chest.

70
00:02:20,692 --> 00:02:22,572
It feels like a sin.

71
00:02:22,923 --> 00:02:24,211
Number two.

72
00:02:24,391 --> 00:02:25,959
We fix things.

73
00:02:26,139 --> 00:02:27,626
When the toaster broke,

74
00:02:27,626 --> 00:02:29,567
my father took it apart on the

75
00:02:29,567 --> 00:02:31,701
kitchen table and fixed it with a

76
00:02:31,701 --> 00:02:32,478
screwdriver.

77
00:02:32,658 --> 00:02:34,970
When my shoe sole came loose,

78
00:02:34,970 --> 00:02:37,283
we took it to the shoe repair

79
00:02:37,283 --> 00:02:38,718
man for a quarter.

80
00:02:38,898 --> 00:02:41,126
When the television went dark,

81
00:02:41,126 --> 00:02:43,206
a man came to the house with

82
00:02:43,206 --> 00:02:44,320
a box of tubes.

83
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:47,598
Nobody threw anything away just because it

84
00:02:47,598 --> 00:02:48,041
broke.

85
00:02:48,221 --> 00:02:49,486
You fixed it.

86
00:02:49,666 --> 00:02:51,992
That was the whole point of owning

87
00:02:51,992 --> 00:02:52,197
it.

88
00:02:52,548 --> 00:02:54,644
So when you tell me to just

89
00:02:54,644 --> 00:02:55,731
buy a new one,

90
00:02:55,731 --> 00:02:59,147
sweetheart, I hear my father's voice saying,

91
00:02:59,147 --> 00:03:01,243
what's wrong with this one?

92
00:03:01,423 --> 00:03:03,607
And honestly, half the time,

93
00:03:03,607 --> 00:03:05,557
nothing is wrong with it.

94
00:03:05,737 --> 00:03:08,128
It just needs a screw tightened.

95
00:03:08,308 --> 00:03:09,887
Try it sometime.

96
00:03:10,067 --> 00:03:12,517
Fix one thing yourself this month.

97
00:03:12,697 --> 00:03:14,880
You'll feel ten feet tall.

98
00:03:15,230 --> 00:03:16,495
Number three.

99
00:03:16,675 --> 00:03:18,370
We are afraid of debt.

100
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:19,607
In my family,

101
00:03:19,607 --> 00:03:22,207
if you couldn't pay cash for it,

102
00:03:22,207 --> 00:03:23,670
you didn't buy it.

103
00:03:23,850 --> 00:03:26,277
My father bought our house with a

104
00:03:26,277 --> 00:03:28,188
mortgage from the GI Bill,

105
00:03:28,188 --> 00:03:29,218
after the war,

106
00:03:29,218 --> 00:03:31,350
and he paid it off five years

107
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:33,115
early and threw a party.

108
00:03:33,295 --> 00:03:34,713
Our first car,

109
00:03:34,713 --> 00:03:36,840
our first television,

110
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:39,169
our first refrigerator.

111
00:03:39,349 --> 00:03:40,592
All cash.

112
00:03:40,772 --> 00:03:41,997
My father said,

113
00:03:41,997 --> 00:03:44,610
a man who owes money doesn't own

114
00:03:44,610 --> 00:03:45,671
his own life.

115
00:03:46,022 --> 00:03:48,713
The first credit card came out in

116
00:03:48,713 --> 00:03:51,323
nineteen fifty, the Diners Club,

117
00:03:51,323 --> 00:03:52,628
for restaurants.

118
00:03:52,808 --> 00:03:56,186
Then BankAmericard in nineteen fifty-eight.

119
00:03:56,366 --> 00:03:58,607
My father would never have one.

120
00:03:58,787 --> 00:04:01,099
He paid for everything in cash,

121
00:04:01,099 --> 00:04:03,485
from an envelope in his dresser,

122
00:04:03,485 --> 00:04:05,126
until the day he died.

123
00:04:05,306 --> 00:04:06,896
I have one card now,

124
00:04:06,896 --> 00:04:09,600
sweetheart, and I pay it off every

125
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:10,078
month.

126
00:04:10,258 --> 00:04:11,278
If I didn't,

127
00:04:11,278 --> 00:04:13,404
I'd hear him in my sleep.

128
00:04:13,754 --> 00:04:15,274
Number four.

129
00:04:15,454 --> 00:04:17,033
We don't complain.

130
00:04:17,213 --> 00:04:18,781
Or we try not to.

131
00:04:18,961 --> 00:04:20,696
When you scraped your knee,

132
00:04:20,696 --> 00:04:21,661
my mother said,

133
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:22,432
you'll live.

134
00:04:22,612 --> 00:04:24,145
When you were sad,

135
00:04:24,145 --> 00:04:25,677
she said, chin up.

136
00:04:25,857 --> 00:04:27,449
When you were bored,

137
00:04:27,449 --> 00:04:30,554
she said, only boring people get bored,

138
00:04:30,554 --> 00:04:31,430
go outside.

139
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:34,849
Nobody asked how we felt about things.

140
00:04:35,029 --> 00:04:37,191
Our fathers had come home from a

141
00:04:37,191 --> 00:04:39,555
war, and they didn't talk about how

142
00:04:39,555 --> 00:04:40,230
they felt.

143
00:04:40,410 --> 00:04:41,850
So neither did we.

144
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:45,111
I won't pretend that was all good,

145
00:04:45,111 --> 00:04:45,624
honey.

146
00:04:45,804 --> 00:04:47,710
A lot of people from my time

147
00:04:47,710 --> 00:04:50,704
carried things inside them their whole lives

148
00:04:50,704 --> 00:04:52,202
and never said a word.

149
00:04:52,382 --> 00:04:54,390
Men who drank too much.

150
00:04:54,570 --> 00:04:57,280
Women who were sad behind the kitchen

151
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:57,647
door.

152
00:04:57,827 --> 00:05:00,384
Your generation talks about feelings,

153
00:05:00,384 --> 00:05:02,180
and I think that's better.

154
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:03,823
I really do.

155
00:05:04,003 --> 00:05:06,429
But we got through a great deal,

156
00:05:06,429 --> 00:05:08,020
keeping our chins up.

157
00:05:08,371 --> 00:05:09,636
Number five.

158
00:05:09,816 --> 00:05:11,232
We know how to wait.

159
00:05:11,412 --> 00:05:13,015
Everything took time.

160
00:05:13,195 --> 00:05:15,679
You ordered from the Sears catalog and

161
00:05:15,679 --> 00:05:16,921
waited three weeks.

162
00:05:17,101 --> 00:05:19,120
You sent a letter and waited a

163
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:20,399
week for an answer.

164
00:05:20,579 --> 00:05:23,381
Your favorite television show was on once

165
00:05:23,381 --> 00:05:25,500
a week, Tuesday night at eight,

166
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:26,936
and if you missed it,

167
00:05:26,936 --> 00:05:27,893
you missed it.

168
00:05:28,073 --> 00:05:29,338
Forever.

169
00:05:29,518 --> 00:05:31,399
There was no rewinding.

170
00:05:31,579 --> 00:05:34,230
You waited for Christmas for a whole

171
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:34,598
year.

172
00:05:34,778 --> 00:05:37,077
And the waiting was half the fun.

173
00:05:37,427 --> 00:05:41,237
Long distance telephone calls were for emergencies

174
00:05:41,237 --> 00:05:41,618
only.

175
00:05:41,798 --> 00:05:44,185
If the phone rang after nine at

176
00:05:44,185 --> 00:05:46,881
night, my mother's face went white,

177
00:05:46,881 --> 00:05:49,576
because it meant somebody had died.

178
00:05:49,756 --> 00:05:52,902
When Grandma Pearl called from Columbus,

179
00:05:52,902 --> 00:05:55,497
my father stood next to the phone

180
00:05:55,497 --> 00:05:56,992
watching the clock,

181
00:05:56,992 --> 00:05:59,823
saying, Ma, this is costing a dollar

182
00:05:59,823 --> 00:06:00,531
a minute.

183
00:06:00,711 --> 00:06:02,974
So we wrote letters instead.

184
00:06:03,154 --> 00:06:04,176
Every week.

185
00:06:04,356 --> 00:06:06,272
Pages and pages.

186
00:06:06,622 --> 00:06:09,432
So when you can't wait four seconds

187
00:06:09,432 --> 00:06:11,038
for a video to load,

188
00:06:11,038 --> 00:06:13,286
sweetheart, I have to smile.

189
00:06:13,466 --> 00:06:15,898
I once waited six months for a

190
00:06:15,898 --> 00:06:18,493
pen pal in France to write back.

191
00:06:18,673 --> 00:06:20,240
Her name was Monique.

192
00:06:20,420 --> 00:06:22,053
When her letter came,

193
00:06:22,053 --> 00:06:23,764
I read it forty times.

194
00:06:23,944 --> 00:06:27,067
You don't read anything forty times anymore.

195
00:06:27,247 --> 00:06:29,963
Maybe you should try it with something

196
00:06:29,963 --> 00:06:30,892
that matters.

197
00:06:31,243 --> 00:06:32,543
Number six.

198
00:06:32,723 --> 00:06:33,849
We are formal.

199
00:06:34,029 --> 00:06:36,827
Every grownup was Mister or Missus.

200
00:06:37,007 --> 00:06:38,133
Mrs.

201
00:06:38,313 --> 00:06:39,660
Pruitt, Mr.

202
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,732
Hoffman, Dr.

203
00:06:41,912 --> 00:06:42,818
Hartman.

204
00:06:42,998 --> 00:06:45,041
You would no more call a grownup

205
00:06:45,041 --> 00:06:47,021
by their first name than fly to

206
00:06:47,021 --> 00:06:47,596
the moon.

207
00:06:47,776 --> 00:06:50,237
You said yes ma'am and no sir.

208
00:06:50,417 --> 00:06:52,856
You stood up when an older person

209
00:06:52,856 --> 00:06:54,260
came into the room.

210
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:56,825
And you wrote a thank-you note for

211
00:06:56,825 --> 00:06:58,299
every single present,

212
00:06:58,299 --> 00:07:00,193
by hand, before New Year's,

213
00:07:00,193 --> 00:07:02,298
or you didn't get to play with

214
00:07:02,298 --> 00:07:02,509
it.

215
00:07:02,859 --> 00:07:05,113
And when the telephone rang,

216
00:07:05,113 --> 00:07:06,401
you answered it,

217
00:07:06,401 --> 00:07:08,977
Miller residence, Ruth speaking.

218
00:07:09,157 --> 00:07:11,421
Every child knew to say that.

219
00:07:11,601 --> 00:07:14,287
Your mother laughs when I still answer

220
00:07:14,287 --> 00:07:15,630
that way sometimes.

221
00:07:15,810 --> 00:07:17,660
But it meant something,

222
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:18,143
honey.

223
00:07:18,323 --> 00:07:20,469
It meant that you belonged to a

224
00:07:20,469 --> 00:07:21,854
house, and a family,

225
00:07:21,854 --> 00:07:23,931
and that how you behaved was a

226
00:07:23,931 --> 00:07:25,730
reflection on all of them.

227
00:07:26,081 --> 00:07:27,265
Number seven.

228
00:07:27,445 --> 00:07:29,901
We think children can take care of

229
00:07:29,901 --> 00:07:30,696
themselves.

230
00:07:30,876 --> 00:07:32,153
Because we did.

231
00:07:32,333 --> 00:07:35,166
At nine I walked downtown alone.

232
00:07:35,346 --> 00:07:37,294
At ten I took Danny to the

233
00:07:37,294 --> 00:07:38,643
movies on the bus.

234
00:07:38,823 --> 00:07:41,095
We left in the morning and came

235
00:07:41,095 --> 00:07:43,660
home when the streetlights came on,

236
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:45,858
and nobody knew where we were.

237
00:07:46,038 --> 00:07:47,702
We fell out of trees,

238
00:07:47,702 --> 00:07:48,652
we got lost,

239
00:07:48,652 --> 00:07:50,236
we got into trouble,

240
00:07:50,236 --> 00:07:52,691
and we got ourselves out of it.

241
00:07:53,041 --> 00:07:55,438
So when I see your mother tracking

242
00:07:55,438 --> 00:07:56,637
you on her phone,

243
00:07:56,637 --> 00:07:57,766
I understand it.

244
00:07:57,946 --> 00:08:00,964
The world feels more dangerous now.

245
00:08:01,144 --> 00:08:02,699
But I also think,

246
00:08:02,699 --> 00:08:04,070
she'll be fine.

247
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:06,328
Let her get a little lost.

248
00:08:06,508 --> 00:08:08,714
That's how you learn the way home.

249
00:08:08,894 --> 00:08:11,802
Sweetheart, getting lost was the best thing

250
00:08:11,802 --> 00:08:13,492
that ever happened to me.

251
00:08:13,842 --> 00:08:14,759
Number eight.

252
00:08:14,939 --> 00:08:16,123
We stay.

253
00:08:16,303 --> 00:08:19,205
My father worked at the Westinghouse plant

254
00:08:19,205 --> 00:08:20,657
for thirty-one years.

255
00:08:20,837 --> 00:08:22,369
He got a gold watch.

256
00:08:22,549 --> 00:08:25,319
Your grandfather worked at one place for

257
00:08:25,319 --> 00:08:26,497
most of his life.

258
00:08:26,677 --> 00:08:28,082
We stayed married,

259
00:08:28,082 --> 00:08:29,800
even when it was hard.

260
00:08:29,980 --> 00:08:32,086
We went to the same church,

261
00:08:32,086 --> 00:08:33,880
the same grocery store,

262
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:35,129
the same barber,

263
00:08:35,129 --> 00:08:36,377
for forty years.

264
00:08:36,557 --> 00:08:39,377
Loyalty was the most important thing a

265
00:08:39,377 --> 00:08:40,713
person could have.

266
00:08:41,064 --> 00:08:43,324
Sometimes that was foolish,

267
00:08:43,324 --> 00:08:43,827
honey.

268
00:08:44,007 --> 00:08:46,574
Some people stayed in jobs that ground

269
00:08:46,574 --> 00:08:49,208
them down, and in marriages that should

270
00:08:49,208 --> 00:08:49,951
have ended.

271
00:08:50,131 --> 00:08:52,302
But there was something good in it

272
00:08:52,302 --> 00:08:52,558
too.

273
00:08:52,738 --> 00:08:55,812
When you stay somewhere for forty years,

274
00:08:55,812 --> 00:08:57,811
everybody knows your name.

275
00:08:57,991 --> 00:08:59,457
When my father died,

276
00:08:59,457 --> 00:09:02,461
three hundred people came to his funeral.

277
00:09:02,641 --> 00:09:03,535
Three hundred.

278
00:09:03,715 --> 00:09:06,722
I don't think that happens much anymore.

279
00:09:07,072 --> 00:09:08,233
Number nine.

280
00:09:08,413 --> 00:09:09,620
We dress up.

281
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,304
My mother wore a hat and gloves

282
00:09:12,304 --> 00:09:13,515
to go downtown.

283
00:09:13,695 --> 00:09:15,687
My father wore a tie to the

284
00:09:15,687 --> 00:09:16,203
doctor.

285
00:09:16,383 --> 00:09:19,030
People wore their Sunday best to fly

286
00:09:19,030 --> 00:09:20,133
on an airplane,

287
00:09:20,133 --> 00:09:22,780
sweetheart, and to go to the movies.

288
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:25,828
You dressed up because it showed respect.

289
00:09:26,008 --> 00:09:26,958
For the place,

290
00:09:26,958 --> 00:09:28,655
and for the people there.

291
00:09:28,835 --> 00:09:30,966
Coming to the grocery store in your

292
00:09:30,966 --> 00:09:33,279
pajamas would have been the scandal of

293
00:09:33,279 --> 00:09:33,827
the year.

294
00:09:34,177 --> 00:09:36,340
I still can't go to the doctor

295
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:37,493
in a sweatshirt,

296
00:09:37,493 --> 00:09:38,286
sweetheart.

297
00:09:38,466 --> 00:09:40,478
I put on a blouse and my

298
00:09:40,478 --> 00:09:42,658
good earrings, every time.

299
00:09:42,838 --> 00:09:44,280
Your mother says,

300
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:46,402
Mom, it's just a checkup.

301
00:09:46,582 --> 00:09:48,680
But my mother's voice is in my

302
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:51,408
head, saying, you never know who you'll

303
00:09:51,408 --> 00:09:52,457
meet, Ruth Ann.

304
00:09:52,637 --> 00:09:54,262
And she was right,

305
00:09:54,262 --> 00:09:55,075
you know.

306
00:09:55,255 --> 00:09:57,828
I met your grandfather at a church

307
00:09:57,828 --> 00:10:00,174
social in nineteen sixty-three,

308
00:10:00,174 --> 00:10:02,898
wearing my best blue dress and white

309
00:10:02,898 --> 00:10:03,428
gloves.

310
00:10:03,608 --> 00:10:05,928
If I'd been wearing a sweatshirt,

311
00:10:05,928 --> 00:10:07,474
you might not be here.

312
00:10:07,825 --> 00:10:08,951
Number ten.

313
00:10:09,131 --> 00:10:10,620
We grew up afraid,

314
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:12,358
and it made us tough.

315
00:10:12,538 --> 00:10:15,162
Of polio, every summer.

316
00:10:15,342 --> 00:10:17,095
Of the atomic bomb.

317
00:10:17,275 --> 00:10:19,783
We crawled under our desks at school

318
00:10:19,783 --> 00:10:22,291
and practiced hiding from the end of

319
00:10:22,291 --> 00:10:22,987
the world.

320
00:10:23,167 --> 00:10:25,685
In October of nineteen sixty-two,

321
00:10:25,685 --> 00:10:28,126
during the Cuban Missile Crisis,

322
00:10:28,126 --> 00:10:31,178
I truly thought I'd never turn nineteen.

323
00:10:31,358 --> 00:10:33,211
And then we got up and went

324
00:10:33,211 --> 00:10:34,377
to school anyway.

325
00:10:34,557 --> 00:10:36,507
You learn something from that.

326
00:10:36,687 --> 00:10:39,207
You learn that you keep going.

327
00:10:39,557 --> 00:10:41,890
Every summer there was polio.

328
00:10:42,070 --> 00:10:43,846
Swimming pools closed.

329
00:10:44,026 --> 00:10:47,161
Mothers kept children inside in August.

330
00:10:47,341 --> 00:10:48,785
A boy in my class,

331
00:10:48,785 --> 00:10:51,754
Richard Hale, went to the hospital in

332
00:10:51,754 --> 00:10:54,883
nineteen fifty-two and came back in leg

333
00:10:54,883 --> 00:10:55,445
braces.

334
00:10:55,625 --> 00:10:58,625
Then in April of nineteen fifty-five they

335
00:10:58,625 --> 00:11:01,113
announced the Salk vaccine worked,

336
00:11:01,113 --> 00:11:03,601
and the church bells rang all over

337
00:11:03,601 --> 00:11:04,332
Mansfield.

338
00:11:04,512 --> 00:11:06,933
We lined up at school for our

339
00:11:06,933 --> 00:11:08,936
shots, and nobody cried.

340
00:11:09,116 --> 00:11:10,648
Not one child.

341
00:11:10,828 --> 00:11:12,489
We knew what it meant.

342
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:14,499
And number eleven.

343
00:11:14,679 --> 00:11:17,221
We believed things would get better.

344
00:11:17,401 --> 00:11:19,168
That's the biggest one,

345
00:11:19,168 --> 00:11:20,014
sweetheart.

346
00:11:20,194 --> 00:11:23,283
Our parents had lived through the Depression

347
00:11:23,283 --> 00:11:24,125
and the war,

348
00:11:24,125 --> 00:11:25,178
and after that,

349
00:11:25,178 --> 00:11:27,705
every year was better than the last.

350
00:11:27,885 --> 00:11:28,901
A new car,

351
00:11:28,901 --> 00:11:30,119
a new house,

352
00:11:30,119 --> 00:11:32,657
a television, a vacation.

353
00:11:32,837 --> 00:11:35,776
We truly believed that our children would

354
00:11:35,776 --> 00:11:37,353
have more than we did.

355
00:11:37,533 --> 00:11:40,201
That if you worked hard and played

356
00:11:40,201 --> 00:11:42,084
fair, it would work out.

357
00:11:42,435 --> 00:11:45,052
Not everyone got that promise,

358
00:11:45,052 --> 00:11:45,662
I know.

359
00:11:45,842 --> 00:11:48,625
Not everybody in America in the fifties

360
00:11:48,625 --> 00:11:50,266
was allowed to have it.

361
00:11:50,446 --> 00:11:52,719
But in our little house on Elm

362
00:11:52,719 --> 00:11:54,463
Street, we believed it.

363
00:11:54,643 --> 00:11:56,188
And I still do,

364
00:11:56,188 --> 00:11:57,116
a little.

365
00:11:57,296 --> 00:11:58,294
Even now.

366
00:11:58,474 --> 00:12:01,591
That's why I save the margarine tubs,

367
00:12:01,591 --> 00:12:02,096
honey.

368
00:12:02,276 --> 00:12:05,259
Not because I think things will get

369
00:12:05,259 --> 00:12:05,771
worse.

370
00:12:05,951 --> 00:12:08,760
Because I always thought they'd get better,

371
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:11,373
and somebody might need a margarine tub.

372
00:12:11,723 --> 00:12:13,631
So now you know us a little

373
00:12:13,631 --> 00:12:14,126
better.

374
00:12:14,306 --> 00:12:15,749
We waste nothing,

375
00:12:15,749 --> 00:12:16,937
we fix things,

376
00:12:16,937 --> 00:12:18,041
we hate debt,

377
00:12:18,041 --> 00:12:19,569
we don't complain,

378
00:12:19,569 --> 00:12:21,267
we know how to wait,

379
00:12:21,267 --> 00:12:22,710
we say thank you,

380
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:24,493
we let children roam,

381
00:12:24,493 --> 00:12:26,275
we stay, we dress up,

382
00:12:26,275 --> 00:12:27,464
we keep going,

383
00:12:27,464 --> 00:12:28,737
and we believe.

384
00:12:28,917 --> 00:12:31,296
Tell me in the comments which one

385
00:12:31,296 --> 00:12:32,955
is you, or your mother,

386
00:12:32,955 --> 00:12:34,397
or your grandfather.

387
00:12:34,577 --> 00:12:36,504
I'll bet it's more than one.

388
00:12:36,684 --> 00:12:38,391
Goodnight, sweetheart.

389
00:12:38,571 --> 00:12:41,845
Grandma Ruth will be right here tomorrow.
